From be0af40d4e1fd8c4ad1d05c6afece5de417ff320 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rene van Oostrum <Rene.vanOostrum@amd.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 08:02:34 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Ignore DWARF end_sequence rows when mapping addresses to
 source lines

An end_sequence row carries the first address after a sequence, which is
usually the address of the next function's first row. It was recorded as a
location, and the real row at that address was then skipped as a duplicate,
so the start of a function (up to its next line-table row) was attributed to
the previous function's last line. Seen on gfx950: a kernel's ds_write2_b64
instructions mapped to another kernel's line 78 instead of 121.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/addressMap.cc | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/addressMap.cc b/src/addressMap.cc
index e14cadb..d376cc9 100644
--- a/src/addressMap.cc
+++ b/src/addressMap.cc
@@ -344,6 +344,15 @@ bool buildDwarfAddressMap(const char* filename, size_t offset, size_t hsaco_leng
                     continue;
                 }
 
+                // An end_sequence row marks the first address *after* a
+                // sequence; the next function's first row often has the same
+                // address. Recording it attributed the start of that function to
+                // the previous function's last line (and the same-address check
+                // below then dropped the real row).
+                Dwarf_Bool end_sequence = 0;
+                if (dwarf_lineendsequence(linebuf[i], &end_sequence, &err) == DW_DLV_OK && end_sequence)
+                    continue;
+
                 // Skip if address is zero or same as previous (avoid duplicates)
                 if (addr == 0 || addr == prev_addr) {
                     prev_addr = addr;
-- 
